    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.117 WN19-CC-000240"
      info        : "Windows Server 2019 administrator accounts must not be enumerated during elevation.

GROUP ID:V-205714
RULE ID:SV-205714r958518

Enumeration of administrator accounts when elevating can provide part of the logon information to an unauthorized user. This setting configures the system to always require users to type in a username and password to elevate a running application."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Credential User Interface >> \'Enumerate administrator accounts on elevation\' to \'Disabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53r5|SC-3,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001084,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-205714r958518_rule,STIG-ID|WN19-CC-000240"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22176"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "0"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Credui"
      reg_item    : "EnumerateAdministrators"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.118 WN19-CC-000250"
      info        : 'Windows Server 2019 Telemetry must be configured to Security or Basic.

GROUP ID:V-205869
RULE ID:SV-205869r991589

Some features may communicate with the vendor, sending system information or downloading data or components for the feature. Limiting this capability will prevent potentially sensitive information from being sent outside the enterprise. The \'Security\' option for Telemetry configures the lowest amount of data, effectively none outside of the Malicious Software Removal Tool (MSRT), Defender, and telemetry client settings. \'Basic\' sends basic diagnostic and usage data and may be required to support some Microsoft services.'
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Data Collection >> \'Allow Telemetry\' to \'Enabled\' with \'0 - Security [Enterprise Only]\' or \'1 - Basic\' selected in \'Options\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-205869r991589_rule,STIG-ID|WN19-CC-000250,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22176"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1" || "0"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\DataCollection"
      reg_item    : "AllowTelemetry"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <report type:"WARNING">
      description : "1.12 WN19-00-000120"
      info        : "Windows Server 2019 must have a host-based intrusion detection or prevention system.

GROUP ID:V-205851
RULE ID:SV-205851r991589

A properly configured Host-based Intrusion Detection System (HIDS) or Host-based Intrusion Prevention System (HIPS) provides another level of defense against unauthorized access to critical servers. With proper configuration and logging enabled, such a system can stop and/or alert for many attempts to gain unauthorized access to resources.

NOTE: Nessus has not performed this check. Please review the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
      solution    : "Install a HIDS or HIPS on each server."
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-205851r991589_rule,STIG-ID|WN19-00-000120,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22176"
    </report>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.120 WN19-CC-000270"
      info        : "Windows Server 2019 Application event log size must be configured to 32768 KB or greater.

GROUP ID:V-205796
RULE ID:SV-205796r958752

Inadequate log size will cause the log to fill up quickly. This may prevent audit events from being recorded properly and require frequent attention by administrative personnel."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Event Log Service >> Application >> \'Specify the maximum log file size (KB)\' to \'Enabled\' with a \'Maximum Log Size (KB)\' of \'32768\' or greater.'
      reference   : "800-53|AU-4,800-53r5|AU-4,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001849,CSF|PR.DS-4,CSF|PR.PT-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(b),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.6,ITSG-33|AU-4,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T3.3.1,NESA|T3.6.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-205796r958752_rule,STIG-ID|WN19-CC-000270"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22176"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "[32768..MAX]"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\EventLog\Application"
      reg_item    : "MaxSize"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

